WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.791
Idea. Imagine swapping every letter of a secret note for a little shape. Only a friend with the same key can swap them back. That is a substitution cipher. Here is the word HELLO written in the Pigpen cipher.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:27.320
The key is a set of grids. The first is a noughts-and-crosses grid holding A to I. A letter's symbol is made of the lines around its cell. E sits in the centre, so its symbol is a full square.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:39.489
The second grid is the same shape, holding J to R, but every symbol from it has a dot inside. J is top left, so its symbol is two lines, right and bottom, with a dot.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:54.350
The last two parts are X shapes. S to V sit in a plain X, and W to Z in a dotted X: top, left, right and bottom. T is in the left triangle, so its two lines make an arrowhead pointing right.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:01:06.951
Method. To encrypt a letter, find it in the key. Note which grid it is in, and its position. Draw only the lines that border its cell. Add a dot if it is in a dotted grid.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:18.514
To decrypt, work backwards. Match the outline and the dot to a cell, and read its letter. To judge a key, check every letter has a symbol, and no symbol is used twice.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:32.576
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper 2 in June 2022. Figure one shows a Pigpen key. Give the symbol for each letter in the word MAY. It is worth three marks.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:44.752
M is in the dotted grid, middle row, left cell. It has a line above, a line below and a line on its right. So its symbol is a box open on the left, with a dot inside.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:55.417
A is in the plain grid, top-left cell. It has a line on its right and a line below. So its symbol is a corner made of those two lines, with no dot.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:05.978
Y is in the dotted X, right-hand triangle. Its two lines meet at the centre, on its left, like an arrowhead pointing left, with a dot inside.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:17.141
So the word MAY is written with these three symbols. Each correct symbol earns one mark. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:29.922
Common mistake. A common mistake is forgetting the dot. Look at these two symbols. The first is D, from the plain grid. The second is M. The only difference is the dot.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:38.746
Your turn. Now you try. Using the same key, draw the Pigpen symbols for the word TREE. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:55.773
Answer. T is in the plain X, left triangle: an arrowhead pointing right, no dot. R is in the dotted grid, bottom right: lines on its top and left, with a dot. E is the centre of the plain grid: a full square, drawn twice.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:07.538
Remember the method: find the letter's grid and cell, draw only the lines around that cell, and add a dot for the dotted grids. Now try the practice questions for this type.
